{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0a7182b230c6342","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9004\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9004\n10\t\n11\t## Review Summary\n12\t\n13\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n14\tRecommendation: accept\n15\t\n16\t## Criterion Details\n17\t\n18\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n19\t\n20\tAll three HLRs (HLR-1 P1, HLR-2 P1, HLR-3 P2) map to RHAISTRAT-9004-E001, and the\n21\ttraceability matrix in the decomposition summary is present and accurate. Priority\n22\tinheritance: the epic is P1, correctly derived from the strategy's Jira priority\n23\t(Major → P1) per the docs-authoring exemption — no implementation epics exist to\n24\tinherit from. HLR-3 (P2) is bundled with the P1 guide content, but the strategy is\n25\ttriaged docs-only, so the single-epic bundle is correct by design and priority\n26\tcollapse does not apply. The bundling is also substantively right: the\n27\ttroubleshooting section is a section of the same published guide sharing one\n28\tcontent surface and review cycle.\n29\t\n30\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n31\t\n32\tTriage consistency check passes: `triage: docs-only` with `epic_count: 1`, as\n33\trequired. Single-node DAG with no edges; the epic frontmatter has\n34\t`dependencies: []`, matching the diagram. Critical path length 1 is appropriate\n35\tfor a triaged strategy. No konflux, license-validation, or branch files exist, so\n36\tno parallelism rules apply. No hidden data/artifact dependencies: the only input\n37\t(field enablement docs and the KServe architecture context) is external source\n38\tmaterial, not an epic output, and the summary correctly flags the source-material\n39\tcurrency risk as a health warning with the technical-review AC as the guard.\n40\t\n41\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n42\t\n43\tThe single epic is filed under one component (Documentation) and one team\n44\t(RHOAI Serving, matching the KServe architecture context owning team). KServe is\n45\tcorrectly treated as reference-only. The scope — authoring one guide with five\n46\tsections from existing field enablement source material plus technical review —\n47\tis a coherent single unit of work well within a single sprint. No scope\n48\tduplication (only one epic), and no work-product type mixing: the epic is\n49\tentirely content authoring with review.\n50\t\n51\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n52\t\n53\tThe epic is typed Implementation with `implementation_type: docs-authoring`,\n54\twhich is correct: it produces a concrete artifact (a published guide). No\n55\tInvestigation epic exists, and none is needed — the strategy explicitly states\n56\tthere are no open questions. `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` are both null,\n57\tconsistent with no gates. No conditional branches.\n58\t\n59\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n60\t\n61\tThe epic is an Implementation and carries the 9-signal `ai_signals` rubric (not\n62\t`investigation_signals`, which is correctly null). The ai-signals rationale file\n63\texists and each rationale matches its frontmatter value and the strategy\n64\tcontent. Spot-checks against sources: `architecture_claims: 1` is supported —\n65\tthe KServe architecture context confirms raw deployment as the recommended\n66\tdisconnected path, plain Deployments/Services with no mesh dependency, and the\n67\tServing Runtimes dependency, all as the epic claims. `existing_foundation: 1`\n68\tand `open_questions: 1` are justified by the strategy's statement that source\n69\tmaterial exists and open questions are none. `human_process_gates: -1` correctly\n70\treflects the docs content process review. Computed aggregate fields were not\n71\tre-derived, per review scope.\n72\t\n73\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n74\t\n75\tThe epic has five acceptance criteria, each testable and derived from the\n76\tstrategy's HLRs. The rule-